WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.531
`unsafe` Rust is not automatically wrong.

2
00:00:02.611 --> 00:00:06.503
It exists because some valid systems work
cannot be fully checked by the compiler.

3
00:00:06.583 --> 00:00:10.475
The anti-pattern is treating `unsafe` as a
convenience switch when ordinary Rust

4
00:00:10.555 --> 00:00:11.514
feels restrictive.

5
00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:15.317
Unsafe code asks the programmer to uphold
rules that the compiler cannot fully

6
00:00:15.397 --> 00:00:18.161
verify, so the responsibility boundary
must be explicit.

7
00:00:18.241 --> 00:00:21.287
A safety comment should not merely say
that an operation is safe.

8
00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:24.808
It should explain the conditions that make
it valid: pointer alignment,

9
00:00:24.888 --> 00:00:28.667
lifetime, initialization, aliasing,
ownership, mutability, thread access,

10
00:00:28.747 --> 00:00:32.131
allocation responsibility, and any
assumptions about external code.

11
00:00:32.211 --> 00:00:34.355
If the assumptions cannot be stated
clearly,

12
00:00:34.435 --> 00:00:36.353
the unsafe block is not ready for review.

13
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:38.351
FFI boundaries add even more contracts.

14
00:00:38.431 --> 00:00:40.800
Code crossing into C, platform APIs,
device SDKs,

15
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.546
or other language runtimes may depend on
pointer validity, string encoding,

16
00:00:44.626 --> 00:00:48.293
buffer length, callback lifetime,
allocation and deallocation ownership,

17
00:00:48.373 --> 00:00:52.265
error conventions, and whether functions
are safe to call from multiple threads.

18
00:00:52.345 --> 00:00:54.714
These are design facts, not comments to
add later.

19
00:00:54.794 --> 00:00:58.517
The most reviewable unsafe code is small,
justified, documented, and tested.

20
00:00:58.597 --> 00:01:01.304
It should be isolated near the boundary
that requires it,

21
00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:03.753
surrounded by safe abstractions where
possible,

22
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:07.161
and supported by tests that exercise
normal, edge, and failure cases.

23
00:01:07.241 --> 00:01:11.134
The safe wrapper should prevent ordinary
callers from violating the assumptions

24
00:01:11.214 --> 00:01:12.849
required by the unsafe internals.

25
00:01:12.929 --> 00:01:16.596
A defensive review asks why unsafe is
necessary, what invariant it relies on,

26
00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:19.778
how that invariant is established, how it
is preserved over time,

27
00:01:19.858 --> 00:01:22.171
and what would break if the external API
changes.

28
00:01:22.251 --> 00:01:26.030
Unsafe code should be rare enough and
clear enough that reviewers can spend real

29
00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:26.900
attention on it.
